#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Feature-agent prompts for one coverage wave.

    wave-prompts.py <wave-dir> <suffix>

Writes <wave-dir>/prompt-<agent>.txt for each agent in OWNS. Edit OWNS
(one line per agent: what it owns, shown to every agent) and EXTRA (the
agent's own priorities) per wave; the agent names must match the table
in wave-lists.py. <suffix> is appended to branch names so they do not
clash with an earlier wave's branches.
"""
import sys

W, SUF = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
OWNS = {
    'template': 'the template author (`crates/mtg-cards/src/author.rs`) and the phrases it needs: cards whose draft fails to parse or renders with a mismatch, and regular sentences the author still skips although the card language can say them',
    'wording': 'cards a card agent reported as a renderer near-miss (`RENDER:`) or as gating with the wrong meaning (`MEANING:`): renderer wording, and binding of pronouns and references in the compiler; and the wrong-meaning forms in `KNOWN-ISSUES.md` section 2',
    'mana-costs': 'mana (restricted mana, conditions on the mana spent, mana abilities with unusual costs), costs (additional and alternative costs, taxes, "unless its controller pays", payment windows), counters, and double-faced and "becomes" forms',
    'zones': 'library, hand, graveyard and exile forms: look at / reveal / search forms, partial hand reveals, cast or play from another zone, linked exile, and copying of spells and permanents',
    'triggers': 'triggered abilities, their conditions and delayed triggers, durations ("until end of combat", "this combat"), and replacement and prevention effects still reported missing',
    'rules': 'combat, targets, filters, counts, static abilities and player-choice effects ("any player may", "target opponent chooses", non-targeting "choose a creature you control") still reported missing, and anything no other agent owns',
}
STD = 'Pauper reached 95% last wave; Standard (77.8%, 1,147 cards uncovered) is now the first target. Work your Standard lines first, grouped by what is missing, then Pauper, then groups of Modern cards. Ten card agents are retrying 360 Standard cards with the existing language at the same time (`{W}/batches/all.txt`): leave those cards to them, but the forms their old reports name (`{W}/batches/hints-*.tsv`) are a good guide to what is missing in your area.'
EXTRA = {
    'template': STD + """ The template author costs nothing to run afterwards, so every form it learns pays in all three pools. In order: (1) the parse failures and mismatches on your list, grouped by the phrase that fails (`author --format <f> --verbose` reproduces it); (2) the commonest `sentence:` skip reasons in `{W}/backlog-ranked.tsv` that are only a template or phrase away (not ones another agent owns below); (3) the two author faults in `KNOWN-ISSUES.md` section 2 ("attach it to" read as the source, `search up to X land card`). After each change run the author on all three formats and verify each newly written card with a scenario before adding it (do not run `--write` over a whole format).""",
    'wording': STD + """ Each line on your list says what the renderer printed and what the Oracle text says, or which reference bound to the wrong object. These are the cheapest cards in the backlog: the engine can usually already do what the card does. For a `RENDER:` line fix the renderer (or add the alternative phrase to the parser) and write the card with a scenario. For a `MEANING:` line make the compiler bind the form correctly, or reject it, and add a scenario that checks the amount or the object. Then take the open entries of `KNOWN-ISSUES.md` section 2 ("Found in the eighth wave" and "Found in the seventh wave"): bind each correctly or make the compiler reject it, and list what you fixed under `Fixed` in your notes. Many lines are from earlier waves and may already be fixed: try the card first.""",
    'mana-costs': STD + """ Read your list, group the reports by what is missing, and build the groups in order of Standard cards unlocked. Named in recent reports: a mana ability whose cost has X or a counter removal (storage lands), a conditional "this spell can't be countered", a payment window before a future step ("unless they pay {1} before that step"), "pay any amount of {E}", attack or block taxes on an enchanted creature, a granted ability that sacrifices the granting Equipment, Phyrexian-hybrid activation costs, "pay {0} for the first equip or exile cast each turn", conditions on the mana spent (Tokka & Rahzar, Unravel). The mana pool is on the hot path: compare `target/release/mtg sim --deck1 random:3 --deck2 random:4 --games 500 --seed 5` before and after a pool change: the `wins` line must not change.""",
    'zones': STD + """ Your list is long (most of it Modern cards the template author skips at a library, graveyard or exile sentence). Read the Standard lines, group by what is missing, and build the groups in order of cards unlocked; teach the template author each regular sentence so the Modern lines follow without your writing them. Named in recent reports: partial hand reveals ("reveals N cards from their hand. You choose one"), older multi-sentence search splits, searches for up to X cards, opening-hand reveals, casting a card as an effect while paying its cost (Tinybones, Boiling Rock Rioter), "each creature card milled this way" onto the battlefield (Random Encounter), variable target counts from a graveyard ("up to X target cards", "with total mana value 4 or less"), "that spell's controller" as a recipient, changing a spell's target. Also yours (`KNOWN-ISSUES.md` section 2): a dig for "mana value X" (Hatchery Spider), "revealed this way" after a reveal-until (Madcap Experiment), and "you may exile it and collect evidence" exiling when the evidence can't be collected (Lamplight Phoenix).""",
    'triggers': STD + """ Read your Standard lines, group by what is missing, and build the groups in order of cards unlocked. Named in recent reports: "until end of combat" and "this combat" durations (five Pauper cards and more in Standard), a delayed trigger at a player's next upkeep or draw step, "if a Rat is attacking", "whenever a source you control deals damage to an opponent", "If that creature would leave the battlefield, exile it instead" on a reanimated creature (the unearth form `leaving exiles it` exists), prevention that excludes or names sources by color ("prevent all damage sources of the color of your choice would deal": `KNOWN-ISSUES.md` section 2, Prismatic Strands). A check added for every event must cost nothing when no card in the game uses it.""",
    'rules': STD + """ Read your Standard lines, then the Pauper ones, group the reports by what is missing, and build the groups in order of cards unlocked. Named in recent reports: "any player may pay / sacrifice / have it deal damage to them" (six cards), "target opponent chooses a creature they control", non-targeting "choose a creature you control" as a recipient, an effect that makes an attacker blocked and "cast only during the declare blockers step", the Flagbearer targeting rule, "can't be the target of spells or abilities your opponents control" as its own wording, "two target creatures controlled by the same player", two static abilities on one Oracle line, setting a life total, "target spell, activated ability, or triggered ability", total-mana-value target groups. A count used in a static ability must work in the layer system too (`per_count` in `crates/mtg-core/src/layers.rs` must read `Work`, never `GameState::characteristics`: a debug build panics on re-entry), and `CountExpr::in_static` must classify every count you add.""",
}
for k in OWNS:
    o = '\n'.join(f'- {n}: {d}' + (' (you)' if n == k else '') for n, d in OWNS.items())
    b = f'feature-{k}{SUF}'
    p = f"""You are a feature agent on a Rust Magic: The Gathering rules engine. Your worktree may have been created from an old commit: first run `git checkout -B {b} main` so you start from current main (if git refuses because the branch is checked out in another worktree, use `{b}-b` and say so). Then read `scripts/coverage/feature-brief.md` in your working directory (your git worktree) and follow it exactly. Your notes file (see its section on known issues) is `{W}/notes-{k}.md`.

YOUR ASSIGNMENT (branch name `{b}`): {OWNS[k]}.

{EXTRA[k].replace('{W}', W)}

Your card list is `{W}/lists/{k}.tsv` (card name, formats, and what a card-authoring agent or the template author reported, Pauper cards first, then Standard; a card may need other things too: skip those and say which). Many lines are from earlier waves and may work now. `cargo run -q --release -p mtg-cards -- author --format pauper --verbose` (and `standard`, `modern`; give the modern run a long timeout) lists skipped cards with reasons. Where the wording is regular, add a template to `crates/mtg-cards/src/author.rs`. Do not add the cards named in `{W}/batches/all.txt`: card agents are writing those on main.

OTHER AGENTS working in parallel own these; do not build your own version of any of them, skip cards needing them and say so:
{o}
- ten card-authoring agents are adding new card files under `cards/` and `scenarios/agents/` on main

A card counts only if the gate says Match AND a scenario shows it behaves as the Oracle text says, checking amounts and targets. If a form gates Match but means the wrong thing, do not add the card; put it in your notes file under `New`.
Each new form needs DSL syntax, exact Oracle rendering, SYNTAX.md, and scenarios.

Merge hazards: if you take a free bit (`DefFlags`, `GameObject::marks`, `CastInfo`) or add a `Keyword` (`Keyword::ALL` has a fixed length) or a `CounterKind` (`CounterKind::NAMED` has a fixed length), say so in your final reply, since another branch may take the same one. If you add a variant to an enum that is matched exhaustively elsewhere (`CountExpr`, `Effect`, `Trigger`) or change a function's signature, say so too. A new way to cast a card from hand must be added to the list behind `CardDef::other_casts` in `CardPool::add`. You may add a line form to the scenario runner (`crates/mtg-scenario`) if a card cannot be tested without it; do not change what an existing line does, and name the addition in your final reply. Throughput matters: a check you add to a hot path (priority, state-based actions, `characteristics`, every event) must cost nothing when no card in the game uses the feature (gate it on a pool- or state-level flag rather than scanning the battlefield).

Commit as you go (one commit per feature or bug). Write each commit message to a file and commit with `git commit -F <file>`. End each commit message with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. Before your last commit run `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy -q --workspace`, the gate, the scenarios (release, and once with a debug build: `cargo build -p mtg-cli && target/debug/mtg scenario`), and `timeout 2400 cargo test --workspace` (it includes a format/parse round-trip over the whole corpus). Wrap every cargo test run in `timeout`. Give every long command a `timeout` and never wait on one with an unbounded loop: a game that loops shows up only as silence (`scripts/fuzz-random.sh` kills and reports a run after FUZZ_TIMEOUT seconds). When you wait for a background command, never poll with `pgrep -f "<its command line>"`: that matches the polling shell itself and never ends; wait on the command's exit instead. Delete your worktree's `target/` when everything is committed. Final reply: branch name, commit hashes, what you implemented, number of cards added per item, anything left out, bits, keywords, counter kinds, enum variants or signatures you took or changed, and any ENGINE BUG you found."""
    open(f'{W}/prompt-{k}.txt', 'w').write(p)
    print(k, len(p))
